Multi-Tenant Kubernetes Architecture (Local Development)
This repository contains the configuration for a multi-tenant Kubernetes architecture running locally on macOS using Rancher Desktop (K3s).

🏗️ Architecture Overview
Ingress Controller: A single shared ingress-nginx controller handles all incoming traffic and terminates TLS.

TLS Management: cert-manager automatically requests certificates from Let's Encrypt (Staging) using HTTP-01 challenges.

Multi-Tenancy: Tenants are isolated in their own namespaces (user1, user2).

Network Security: NetworkPolicy denies all cross-tenant traffic. Pods can only be reached by the ingress-nginx namespace.

High Availability: Deployments use 2 replicas with /readyz and /healthz probes to manage endpoint routing and container restarts.

🛠️ Prerequisites
Rancher Desktop installed on macOS.

kubectl installed.

🚀 Step-by-Step Setup
Step 1: Prepare Rancher Desktop
Open Rancher Desktop Preferences -> Kubernetes.

Uncheck "Enable Traefik" (we use NGINX instead).

Click Apply and wait for the cluster to restart.

Step 2: Install NGINX Ingress Controller
bash
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/ingress-nginx/controller-v1.10.1/deploy/static/provider/cloud/deploy.yaml
Wait for the ingress-nginx-controller pod to be Running.

Step 3: Expose Ports (macOS requires sudo for ports < 1024)
Open two separate terminal windows and run these commands. Keep them running in the background.

bash
# Terminal 1 (HTTP)
sudo kubectl port-forward -n ingress-nginx service/ingress-nginx-controller 80:80

# Terminal 2 (HTTPS)
sudo kubectl port-forward -n ingress-nginx service/ingress-nginx-controller 443:443
Step 4: Install cert-manager
bash
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/cert-manager/cert-manager/releases/download/v1.15.4/cert-manager.yaml
Wait for all 3 pods in the cert-manager namespace to be Running.

Step 5: Configure Let's Encrypt ClusterIssuer
Create a file clusterissuer.yaml (replace the email with your real email):

yaml
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: letsencrypt-staging
spec:
  acme:
    server: https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
    email: your-email@example.com # Must not be example.com
    privateKeySecretRef:
      name: letsencrypt-staging
    solvers:
      - http01:
          ingress:
            ingressClassName: nginx
Apply it:

bash
kubectl apply -f clusterissuer.yaml
Verify it says READY: True (kubectl get clusterissuer).

Step 6: Deploy the Tenants
Create and apply user1-demo.yaml and user2-demo.yaml. (These files include Namespace, NetworkPolicy, Deployment, Service, and Ingress).

bash
kubectl apply -f user1-demo.yaml
kubectl apply -f user2-demo.yaml
Step 7: Configure Local DNS
Map the tenant domains to your localhost by editing your Mac's hosts file:

bash
sudo nano /etc/hosts
Add the following lines:

text
127.0.0.1 user1.localhost
127.0.0.1 user2.localhost
Save and exit (Ctrl+O, Enter, Ctrl+X).

🧪 Validation & Testing
1. Test Ingress & TLS
Go to http://user1.localhost -> Should see the Nginx welcome page.

Go to https://user1.localhost -> Should see the Nginx page, but with a "Not Secure" warning. (This is expected because we are using Let's Encrypt Staging certificates).

2. Test NetworkPolicy Isolation
Prove that user2 cannot communicate with user1 directly:

bash
kubectl exec -it deployment/web-app -n user2 -- sh
Inside the pod, try to reach user1:

bash
wget -qO- --timeout=3 http://web-service.user1.svc.cluster.local
Expected Result: Connection refused or Timeout (Isolation works!).

Test outbound internet access:

bash
wget -qO- --timeout=3 https://google.com
Expected Result: HTML of Google (Egress works!).

Type exit to leave the pod.

🧹 Cleanup
To remove all resources and stop the setup:

bash
kubectl delete ns user1 user2
Press Ctrl+C in the terminal windows running the port-forward commands to stop routing local traffic.





Multi-Tenant Kubernetes Architecture with CI/CD (M2)
This repository contains a fully automated, event-driven, multi-tenant Kubernetes architecture. It simulates a real-world CI/CD pipeline and includes a Kafka event bus for deployment notifications.

🏗️ Architecture Overview
CI/CD Pipeline (ci-pipeline.sh): Automates Docker builds, security scanning, pushing to a local registry, and Helm deployments.

Security Gate: Uses Trivy to scan images for vulnerabilities.

Helm: Dynamically deploys tenants using a reusable chart and tenant-specific values.yaml files.

Health Gate: Runs a curl / smoke test after deployment. If it fails, it triggers a helm rollback.

Event Broker (Kafka): Publishes WebsiteCreated events on success.

Event Consumer ("notify"): A Python application that listens to Kafka, ensures idempotency (using tenant-sha keys), and simulates Slack/Email notifications. Includes Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) logic for failures.

🛠️ Prerequisites
Docker (or Rancher Desktop with Docker CLI).

Helm (brew install helm).

Trivy (brew install aquasecurity/trivy/trivy).

A running Kubernetes cluster (Rancher Desktop / K3s) with the ingress-nginx controller installed (from M1).

🚀 Step-by-Step Setup
Step 1: Start the Local Container Registry
We need a local registry to store the images built by the pipeline.

bash
docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name registry registry:2
Step 2: Setup the Helm Chart
Ensure your Helm chart is in charts/web-app/ and your tenant configurations are in tenants/.

tenants/user1.yaml should contain host: user1.localhost

charts/web-app/values.yaml should define the default image, replicas, and ingress settings.

Step 3: Deploy Kafka
We use a local Bitnami Kafka cluster for testing. (Note: We use the bitnamilegacy images to bypass recent Bitnami licensing changes).

bash
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
helm repo update

helm install kafka bitnami/kafka \
  --set replicaCount=1 \
  --set zookeeper.enabled=true \
  --set persistence.enabled=false \
  --set zookeeper.persistence.enabled=false \
  --set global.security.allowInsecureImages=true \
  --set image.registry=docker.io \
  --set image.repository=bitnamilegacy/kafka \
  --set zookeeper.image.registry=docker.io \
  --set zookeeper.image.repository=bitnamilegacy/zookeeper \
  --set listeners.client.protocol=PLAINTEXT
Wait for the kafka-controller-0 and kafka-zookeeper-0 pods to show Running.

Step 4: Deploy the Kafka Consumer
The consumer is located in the consumer/ folder.

Build and push the consumer image:

bash
docker build --no-cache -t localhost:5000/notify-consumer:v1 ./consumer
docker push localhost:5000/notify-consumer:v1
Deploy the consumer to Kubernetes:

bash
kubectl apply -f consumer-deployment.yaml
Step 5: Make the CI Pipeline Executable
Ensure your pipeline script is executable:

bash
chmod +x ci-pipeline.sh
🧪 How to Run and Validate the Pipeline
1. Trigger a Deployment
Run the pipeline for a specific tenant:

bash
./ci-pipeline.sh user1
What happens:

It builds the Docker image and tags it with YYYYMMDD-sha.

Trivy scans it (currently configured to warn but not fail).

Pushes the image to localhost:5000.

Runs helm upgrade --install.

Performs a health check via port-forward and curl.

Publishes a JSON event ({"event":"WebsiteCreated","tenant":"user1"}) to the website.events Kafka topic.

2. Verify the Kafka Event
Check the logs of the Python consumer to see it process the event and simulate the Slack notification:

bash
kubectl logs -l app=notify-consumer
3. Test Idempotency
If you restart the consumer, or if the pipeline pushes the same tenant-sha pair again, you will see the consumer skip it:

text
⏭️ Skipping duplicate event: user1-local
4. Test the Rollback (The "Fail" Path)
To see the pipeline's health gate fail and trigger a Helm rollback:

Temporarily edit app/index.html to break the web server (or delete the file).

Run ./ci-pipeline.sh user1.

The pipeline will deploy, but the Health Gate (curl) will fail.

The script will automatically trigger helm rollback user1 and publish a deploy.failed event.

🧹 Cleanup
To tear down the M2 resources when you are finished testing:

bash
# Uninstall the apps and Kafka
helm uninstall user1 user2 kafka

# Delete the consumer
kubectl delete deployment notify-consumer
kubectl delete pod kafka-consumer-verify --ignore-not-found

# Delete the namespaces
kubectl delete ns user1 user2

# Stop the local registry
docker stop registry
docker rm registry



Multi-Tenant Kubernetes Architecture with CI/CD & Observability (M2)
This repository contains a fully automated, event-driven, multi-tenant Kubernetes architecture. It includes a local CI/CD pipeline, a Kafka event bus for deployment notifications, Horizontal Pod Autoscaling (HPA), and a Prometheus/Grafana observability stack.

🏗️ Architecture Overview
CI/CD Pipeline (ci-pipeline.sh): Automates Docker builds, Trivy security scanning, pushing to a local registry, and Helm deployments.

Multi-Tenancy: Tenants are isolated in their own namespaces with strict NetworkPolicies. Cross-tenant traffic is denied.

Health Gates & Rollback: Runs a curl smoke test after deployment. If it fails, it triggers an automatic helm rollback.

Event Broker (Kafka): Publishes WebsiteCreated events on success to the website.events topic.

Event Consumer ("notify"): A Python app that listens to Kafka, ensures idempotency (tenant, sha), and simulates Slack notifications.

Autoscaling & Capacity: HPA scales pods based on 50% CPU target. A ResourceQuota acts as a capacity policy, blocking the HPA from overloading the node.

Observability: Prometheus and Grafana monitor cluster health, pod status, and resource saturation.

🛠️ Prerequisites
Docker (or Rancher Desktop with Docker CLI).

Helm (brew install helm).

Trivy (brew install aquasecurity/trivy/trivy).

A running Kubernetes cluster (Rancher Desktop / K3s) with the ingress-nginx controller installed.

metrics-server enabled (Standard in Rancher Desktop).

🚀 Step-by-Step Setup
1. Start the Local Container Registry
bash
docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name registry registry:2
2. Deploy Kafka
We use a local Bitnami Kafka cluster. (Note: We use the bitnamilegacy images to bypass recent Bitnami licensing changes).

bash
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
helm repo update

helm install kafka bitnami/kafka \
  --set replicaCount=1 \
  --set zookeeper.enabled=true \
  --set persistence.enabled=false \
  --set zookeeper.persistence.enabled=false \
  --set global.security.allowInsecureImages=true \
  --set image.registry=docker.io \
  --set image.repository=bitnamilegacy/kafka \
  --set zookeeper.image.registry=docker.io \
  --set zookeeper.image.repository=bitnamilegacy/zookeeper \
  --set listeners.client.protocol=PLAINTEXT
3. Deploy the Kafka Consumer
The consumer ensures idempotency based on the (tenant, sha) key.

bash
docker build --no-cache -t localhost:5000/notify-consumer:v1 ./consumer
docker push localhost:5000/notify-consumer:v1
kubectl apply -f consumer-deployment.yaml
4. Deploy Observability Stack (Prometheus & Grafana)
bash
helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts
helm repo update
helm install monitoring prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack --namespace monitoring --create-namespace
Access Grafana:

Get the admin password:
kubectl get secret --namespace monitoring monitoring-grafana -o jsonpath="{.data.admin-password}" | base64 --decode ; echo

Port-forward:
kubectl port-forward -n monitoring svc/monitoring-grafana 3000:80

Open http://localhost:3000 (User: admin, Password: from step 1).

🧪 Pipeline & Scaling Validation
Test 1: Trigger a Deployment (CI/CD)
Ensure your ci-pipeline.sh has the correct helm upgrade command with the Kafka publish logic, then run:

bash
./ci-pipeline.sh user1
What happens: Builds image ➔ Trivy scans ➔ Pushes to registry ➔ Deploys via Helm ➔ Tests health ➔ Publishes JSON to Kafka.
Verify Kafka: kubectl logs -l app=notify-consumer

Test 2: Test Autoscaling & The "Blocked Path" (Resource Quota)
We configured user1-hpa.yaml (target 50% CPU, max 6 pods) and user1-quota.yaml (max 4 pods).

Apply the Quota and HPA:

bash
kubectl apply -f user1-quota.yaml
kubectl apply -f user1-hpa.yaml
Start Load: Run a load generator in the user1 namespace:

bash
kubectl run load-generator -n user1 --image=fortio/fortio:latest --restart=Never --overrides='{"apiVersion":"v1","spec":{"containers":[{"name":"load-generator","image":"fortio/fortio:latest","resources":{"requests":{"cpu":"50m","memory":"32Mi"},"limits":{"cpu":"100m","memory":"64Mi"}},"args":["load","-c","50","-qps","0","-t","120s","http://web-service"]}]}}'
Watch HPA: kubectl get hpa -n user1 -w

CPU spikes ➔ Replicas increase from 2 to 4.

Observe Quota Block: The HPA wants 6 pods, but the ResourceQuota blocks the 5th pod.

Run kubectl describe quota -n user1 to see pods: 4/4.

Run kubectl get events -n user1 | grep FailedCreate to see the exact quota rejection.

Scale Down: Delete the load generator (kubectl delete pod load-generator -n user1 --grace-period=0 --force). The HPA will wait 5 minutes before scaling back to 2 pods.

Test 3: Test the Rollback (The "Fail" Path)
Break the app (e.g., delete app/index.html).

Run ./ci-pipeline.sh user1.

The Health Gate (curl) fails.

The script triggers helm rollback user1 and prints a deploy.failed event.

🧹 Cleanup
To tear down the M2 resources when you are finished testing:

bash
# Uninstall apps, Kafka, and Observability
helm uninstall user1 user2 kafka monitoring

# Delete consumer and namespaces
kubectl delete deployment notify-consumer
kubectl delete ns user1 user2 monitoring

# Stop the local registry
docker stop registry && docker rm registry
